  - method: Fit power law + Balmer continuum + FeII template + MgII Gaussian by chi2; Vestergaard & Osmer 2009 virial relation; Shen08 bolometric correction [5.6 Black Hole Masses: "We derive the mass of the black hole following (cite: VestergaardOsmer09):"]
  - assumption: Eddington luminosity L_Edd = 1.3e38 M_BH; L_bol = 5.15 lambda L3000 [5.6 Black Hole Masses: "\frac{L_{\mathrm{bol}}}{\mathrm{erg\, s^{-1}}} = 5.15 \times \frac{\lambda L_{\lambda,3000}}{\mathrm{erg\, s^{-1}}}"]
  - fitting: chi2 minimization; 14th/86th percentiles as 1 sigma [5.5 Mg II and Fe II emission modeling: "We consider the 14th and 86th interquartiles of the $\chi^{2}$ distribution as our 1$\sigma$ confidence levels."]
  - validation: Compared with luminosity-matched SDSS sample (1000 random draws) [5.6 Black Hole Masses: "we repeat this trial for 1000 times"]
- result: Mean log L_bol/L_Edd, z>~6.5 quasars = -0.41 ± scatter 0.44 dex [5.6 Black Hole Masses: "$\langle \mathrm{log} (L_{\mathrm{bol}}/L_{\mathrm{Edd}}) \rangle$=-0.41, with a scatter of 0.34 and 0.44"]
- limitation (authors): High-z sample biased to high luminosities [6 Discussion and Conclusions: "We also note that the high$-$redshift sample is biased towards higher luminosities"]
- not stated (card): ALMA data are only used via the Decarli et al. in prep [CII] redshifts; no ALMA calibration or imaging given; NOEMA line-fit windows and continuum map procedure not detailed; Exact SED/spectrum fitting window adjustments for sky lines not given

### 2019ApJ...872..121U: Molecular Cloud Cores in the Galactic Center 50 km s<SUP>-1</SUP> Molecular Cloud (2019; milky_way_ism, star_formation; observational_alma_primary)
- question: Does cloud-cloud collision in the Galactic Center 50 km/s molecular cloud compress gas and form massive bound cores, and how does the core mass function compare with Orion A?
- step 3: Estimate Tex, column density, LTE and virial masses
  - method: RADEX LVG on C32S 1-0 and 2-1 for Tex per core; LTE N(H2) from line intensities; mass sums; virial mass 210 R dV^2; alpha = Mvir/MLTE [4.2. Mass estimation: "we used the T ex obtained for each core candidate in the column density estimation"]
  - software: RADEX
  - assumption: X(H13CO+)=6e-11, X(C34S)=4.87e-10, distance 8.5 kpc, mu=2.35e-57 Msun, no external pressure or magnetic field [4.2. Mass estimation: "We estimate the virial masses of the core candidates assuming no external pressure and no magnetic field."]
  - validation: C34S abundance compared to Orion A estimate [4.2. Mass estimation: "This value is consistent with that in the 50MC."]
- result: total LTE mass of 50MC (H13CO+) = 1.3e6 ± factor ~2 abundance Msun [4.2. Mass estimation: "The total LTE mass of the 50MC is 1 . 3 × 10 [6] M ⊙ from the H 13 CO + J = 1 − 0 channel maps"]
- limitation (authors): Abundance uncertainty up to a factor of 2 affects masses [4.2. Mass estimation: "Because the uncertainty of the abundance that is used to estimate the mass is up to a factor of 2"]
- not stated (card): CASA version; clean mask, threshold, multiscale and cell/image size; line-free channels / continuum subtraction; Flux calibrator and flux scale uncertainty; Feathering scale factor / TP calibration; Clumpfind details such as how Tex per core was assigned from LVG grid; Fitting method and binning for CMF power law; Definition of CCC region mask beyond R_T>2.5

### 2020A&A...642A.155Z: GOODS-ALMA: Optically dark ALMA galaxies shed light on a cluster in formation at z = 3.5 (2020; high_z_galaxies, galaxy_clusters_and_sz; observational_alma_primary)
- question: What are the properties of the six optically dark galaxies in the GOODS-ALMA survey, what are their redshifts, and do they trace a proto-cluster at z~3.5?
